problem:
Let
\[f(a,b) = \left\{
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{3}
\begin{array}{cl}
\dfrac{ab - a + 2}{2a} & \text{if $a + b \le 3$}, \\
\dfrac{ab - b - 2}{-2b} & \text{if $a + b > 3$}.
\end{array}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1}
\right.\]Find $f(2,1) + f(2,4).$

solution:
First, it is probably a good idea to sketch our triangle: [asy]
pair A, B, C, M;
A = (0, 5.657);
B = (0, 0);
C = (2, 0);
M = 0.5 * A + 0.5 * C;
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(B--M);
label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$M$", M, NE);
label("$4\sqrt{2}$", A--B, W);
label("$2$", B--C, S);
draw(rightanglemark(A,B,C,10));
[/asy] Using the Pythagorean Theorem, we can find $AC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2 = 32 + 4 = 36,$ so $AC = 6.$ Since $\triangle ABC$ is a right triangle, $BM = \frac{1}{2} \cdot AC = \boxed{3}.$

problem:
Given that

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{x}+\frac{1}{y}&=5,\\
3xy+x+y&=4,
\end{align*}

compute $x^2y+xy^2$.

For rational numbers $a$ and $b$, define the operation "$\otimes$" as $a \otimes b = ab - a - b - 2$.
(1) Calculate the value of $(-2) \otimes 3$;
(2) Compare the size of $4 \otimes (-2)$ and $(-2) \otimes 4$.

solution:
We divide the two given polynomials to obtain \[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{x^3} & +3x^2 & +9x & +28 & +\frac{86}{x-3} \\
\cline{2-6}
x-3 & x^4 & +0x^3 & +0x^2 &+ x &+ 2 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{-x^4} & +3x^3 & \\ \cline{2-3}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 3x^3 & & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & -3x^3 & + 9x^2 & & \\ \cline{3-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 9x^2 & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & -9x^2 &+27x & \\ \cline{4-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & &+28x & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & &-28x & +84 \\ \cline{5-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & 86, \\
\end{array}
\]which shows that the remainder is $\boxed{86}$. Alternatively, we could use the remainder theorem, which states that the remainder when a polynomial $p(x)$ is divided by $x-a$, the remainder is $p(a)$. We find that the remainder is $3^4+3+2=86$.

problem:
Let
\[ f(x) = \left\{ 
\begin{array}{cl} 
2ax + 4, & \text{if } x > 2, \\
x^2-2 & \text{if } -2 \le x \le 2, \\
3x - c, & \text{if } x < -2. 
\end{array} 
\right. \]
Find $a+c$ if the piecewise function is continuous.

solution:
We must draw first! [asy]
pair A, B, C, K;
A = (0, 4.472);
B = (-2.236, 0);
C = (4, 0);
K = (0, 0);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--K);
label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$K$", K, S);
label("9", C--A, NE);
label("$\sqrt{5}$", B--K, S);
label("4", C--K, S);
draw(rightanglemark(A,K,B,10));
[/asy] We first apply the Pythagorean Theorem to $\triangle AKC$ to find that $AK^2 = AC^2 - KC^2 = 9^2 - 4^2 = 65.$ Now, we apply the Pythagorean Theorem to $\triangle AKB$ to find that $AB^2 = AK^2 + BK^2 = 65 + (\sqrt{5})^2 = 70.$ Therefore, $AB = \boxed{\sqrt{70}}.$
